From e03274e090ac82653c25b5fe46a64f0a9f283db9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 22:35:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(android): actually reconnect the runtime socket after a
 GPU crash
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

After the GPU process died the browser logged "GPU process lost;
dropping the adopted runtime socket so the next launch reconnects", but
the relaunched GPU process got "no runtime socket to hand the GPU
process" — inline-3D was inert for the rest of the session.

takeFdForChildProcess() -> ensureConnected() did try to reconnect, so
the reconnect was being ATTEMPTED and failing silently: connectTo()
reuses the retained Client, and a second blockingConnect() on a Client
that already holds a binding is not something the runtime apk's Client
promises to serve. The refusal returned null with nothing logged.

Two changes, belt and braces:

 - ensureConnected() now retries each runtime package twice. Attempt 0
   reuses the retained Client (the warm path, and the one that keeps our
   satellite slot); attempt 1 discards it — calling
   markAsDiscardedByOwner() first when the runtime offers it, so the
   binding and slot are released rather than leaked — and builds a fresh
   one. connectTo() logs the refused return value.

 - onAdopterLost() now posts the reconnect immediately instead of
   leaving it to the next launch. The GPU relaunch lands within
   milliseconds, so this moves the ~300 ms connect off the launcher
   thread; if the launch wins the race, ensureConnected()'s lock makes
   it wait for the in-flight connect rather than start a second one.

Folded in: releasing the CLIENT, not just our dup of the socket. The first cut
of this fix recovered the next GPU process but leaked each dead one's
connection — runtime health showed slots 0 AND 1 as PRESENT_OWNER attributed to
the browser pid from adopters that were already gone, and the third
xrCreateInstance failed -10 (XR_ERROR_LIMIT_REACHED; the PRESENT_OWNER quota is
2, system-wide). onAdopterLost() kept the Client on the documented theory that
the Client owns the slot and the slot should never move. That theory is what
leaks: the Client holds its own fd and its binding, so from the runtime's side
the connection never died and the slot was never reclaimed.
closeConnectionLocked() now drops the socket AND the Client
(markAsDiscardedByOwner() first, best-effort, so the binding and slot go now
rather than at GC), and onAdopterLost() calls it before posting the reconnect —
exactly one live connection per browser process at any moment, and the fresh
Client claims a slot only after the dead one is gone.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_019Lf6vUbdL59MqXMwJxB489
---
 .../displayxr/DisplayXrRuntimeConnector.java  | 92 +++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 74 insertions(+), 18 deletions(-)

diff --git a/components/displayxr/android/java/src/org/chromium/components/displayxr/DisplayXrRuntimeConnector.java b/components/displayxr/android/java/src/org/chromium/components/displayxr/DisplayXrRuntimeConnector.java
index c9ab99ac9b2ac..ab780c60f82d0 100644
--- a/components/displayxr/android/java/src/org/chromium/components/displayxr/DisplayXrRuntimeConnector.java
+++ b/components/displayxr/android/java/src/org/chromium/components/displayxr/DisplayXrRuntimeConnector.java
@@ -37,9 +37,15 @@ import java.lang.reflect.Method;
  * <p>Two lifetime rules from the spec, both load-bearing:
  *
  * <ul>
- *   <li>The Client instance is RETAINED for the browser-process lifetime. It owns the
- *       ServiceConnection, the satellite-slot claim and the slot death-link token; dropping it
- *       unbinds the service and releases the slot out from under the adopter.
+ *   <li>The Client instance is RETAINED while its adopter lives. It owns the ServiceConnection,
+ *       the satellite-slot claim and the slot death-link token; dropping it unbinds the service
+ *       and releases the slot out from under the adopter. It is RELEASED the moment the adopter
+ *       dies — see {@link #onAdopterLost}. Holding it across a GPU crash (which an earlier
+ *       revision did, on the theory that the slot should never move) leaks the connection: the
+ *       runtime sees the Client's own fd still open, keeps the dead adopter's slot as
+ *       PRESENT_OWNER, and the third GPU process fails xrCreateInstance with
+ *       XR_ERROR_LIMIT_REACHED — the PRESENT_OWNER quota is 2, system-wide. Exactly one live
+ *       connection per browser process, always.
  *   <li>What we hand out is always a {@code dup()}. SO_PEERCRED, slot ownership and the death link
  *       all attribute to THIS process (the connector), not the adopter — which is exactly why a GPU
  *       crash does not cost us the slot.
@@ -130,15 +136,50 @@ public class DisplayXrRuntimeConnector {
     @CalledByNative
     private static void onAdopterLost() {
         synchronized (sLock) {
-            if (sSocket == null) return;
+            if (sSocket == null && sClient == null) return;
+            // The Client goes too, not just our dup. Keeping it is what left slots 0 and 1 held
+            // by dead adopters until the runtime refused the third session (-10,
+            // XR_ERROR_LIMIT_REACHED).
+            closeConnectionLocked();
+        }
+        // Reconnect NOW rather than lazily. A GPU relaunch follows within
+        // milliseconds, and its launcher thread reaches takeFdForChildProcess()
+        // before a lazy reconnect would have started — which is exactly how the
+        // second GPU process ended up with "no runtime socket". Doing it here
+        // moves the ~300 ms connect off the launcher thread; if the launch does
+        // beat us to it, ensureConnected()'s lock makes it wait for this one
+        // instead of starting a second connect.
+        Log.i(TAG, "adopter lost; released the runtime connection, reconnecting now");
+        PostTask.postTask(TaskTraits.USER_BLOCKING_MAY_BLOCK, () -> ensureConnected());
+    }
+
+    /**
+     * Release EVERYTHING this process holds for the current runtime connection: our dup of the
+     * socket AND the Client (its own fd, its binding, its PRESENT_OWNER slot). Until both are
+     * gone the runtime cannot observe the connection dying, so the slot is never reclaimed.
+     *
+     * <p>Caller must hold {@link #sLock}.
+     */
+    private static void closeConnectionLocked() {
+        if (sSocket != null) {
             try {
                 sSocket.close();
             } catch (Exception e) {
-                Log.w(TAG, "closing the stale runtime socket failed", e);
+                Log.w(TAG, "closing the runtime socket failed", e);
             }
             sSocket = null;
         }
-        Log.i(TAG, "adopter lost; the next child launch will get a fresh socket");
+        if (sClient != null) {
+            // Best-effort: this is the runtime apk's API, not ours to depend on. Dropping the
+            // reference without it still lets the binding die with our last strong ref, it just
+            // takes until GC rather than now.
+            try {
+                sClient.getClass().getMethod("markAsDiscardedByOwner").invoke(sClient);
+            } catch (Exception e) {
+                Log.w(TAG, "markAsDiscardedByOwner failed; dropping the Client anyway", e);
+            }
+            sClient = null;
+        }
     }
 
     private static @Nullable ParcelFileDescriptor ensureConnected() {
@@ -146,17 +187,29 @@ public class DisplayXrRuntimeConnector {
             if (sSocket != null) return sSocket;
             Context appContext = ContextUtils.getApplicationContext();
             for (String pkg : RUNTIME_PACKAGES) {
-                ParcelFileDescriptor socket;
-                try {
-                    socket = connectTo(appContext, pkg);
-                } catch (Exception e) {
-                    Log.w(TAG, "connect via " + pkg + " failed", e);
-                    continue;
-                }
-                if (socket != null) {
-                    sSocket = socket;
-                    Log.i(TAG, "connected via " + pkg + ", fd " + socket.getFd());
-                    return socket;
+                // Attempt 0 reuses the retained Client (the warm path, and the
+                // one that keeps our satellite slot). Attempt 1 rebuilds it: a
+                // RE-connect on a Client that already holds a binding is not
+                // something the runtime's Client promises to support, and a
+                // reconnect that fails silently is precisely what left the
+                // relaunched GPU process with no socket.
+                for (int attempt = 0; attempt < 2; ++attempt) {
+                    ParcelFileDescriptor socket = null;
+                    try {
+                        socket = connectTo(appContext, pkg);
+                    } catch (Exception e) {
+                        Log.w(TAG, "connect via " + pkg + " attempt " + attempt + " failed", e);
+                    }
+                    if (socket != null) {
+                        sSocket = socket;
+                        Log.i(TAG, "connected via " + pkg + ", fd " + socket.getFd()
+                                + (attempt == 0 ? "" : " (fresh Client)"));
+                        return socket;
+                    }
+                    if (sClient == null) break; // nothing retained to invalidate
+                    // Let go of the stale Client so attempt 1 builds a fresh one, releasing its
+                    // slot rather than stranding it.
+                    closeConnectionLocked();
                 }
             }
             return null;
@@ -182,7 +235,10 @@ public class DisplayXrRuntimeConnector {
         Method blockingConnect =
                 clientClass.getMethod("blockingConnect", Context.class, String.class);
         Object result = blockingConnect.invoke(client, appContext, pkg);
-        if (!(result instanceof Integer) || (Integer) result < 0) return null;
+        if (!(result instanceof Integer) || (Integer) result < 0) {
+            Log.w(TAG, "blockingConnect(" + pkg + ") refused: " + result);
+            return null;
+        }
 
         // Client owns `fd`; take our own reference so the handoff and the Client lifetime are
         // independent.
